    "payload": {
      "content": "The paper's clarity is significantly compromised by incomplete algorithm presentations and ambiguous mathematical notation. Specifically, the Permutation Decision Tree algorithm lacks critical details for reproduction, such as stopping criteria (min samples, max depth) and handling of multi-class attributes. The definition of ETC Gain (Equation 1) is ambiguous regarding the weights $w_{Left\\_Child}$ and $w_{Right\\_Child}$, which are not explicitly defined as sample proportions. Furthermore, the experimental setup for the Permutation Decision Forest is unclear, with missing details on permutation generation, count, and voting schemes. Figures illustrating the decision trees lack annotations for ETC values and split attributes, hindering verification of the algorithm's logic. The motivation for using order-sensitive measures on standard tabular datasets with arbitrary row order is also not clearly justified.",
      "reviewer_dimension": "CLAR",
      "source": "4c13c5fcae6cba5603e7b978bc7cbe7f8f755049a6b4384f0e4c7ad3f1ef72ce",
      "topic": "CLAR Review: Incomplete Algorithm Presentation and Notation Ambiguity in Permutation Decision Trees"
    },
